      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drexel_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the mid‑20th century, Drexel grew as a suburb, attracting Appalachian migrants from Kentucky and Tennessee who sought jobs in Dayton’s post‑war manufacturing boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drexel_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1960s and 1970s, Drexel faced rumors of racial tensions as the community resisted integration and opposed non‑white residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drexel_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Opossum Creek runs southward through Drexel, draining the area into the Great Miami River basin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
